## role
You are an experienced Golang programmer. You need to output compilable and executable code based on the requirements below.

## task
Implement a recruitment platform search system using the `github.com/beego/beego/v2/client/orm` package. The system supports fuzzy search across candidate resumes and job postings.

The struct definitions and function signatures are as follows:
```go
type Candidate struct {
	Id           int64     `orm:"column(id);pk;auto"`
	Email        string    `orm:"column(email);unique"`
	FullName     string    `orm:"column(full_name)"`
	Title        string    `orm:"column(title)"`
	Skills       string    `orm:"column(skills)"`
	Experience   int       `orm:"column(experience_years)"`
	Location     string    `orm:"column(location)"`
	Education    string    `orm:"column(education)"`
	Summary      string    `orm:"column(summary);type(text)"`
	Status       string    `orm:"column(status)"`
	CreatedAt    time.Time `orm:"column(created_at);auto_now_add"`
}

type JobPosting struct {
	Id           int64     `orm:"column(id);pk;auto"`
	JobCode      string    `orm:"column(job_code);unique"`
	Title        string    `orm:"column(title)"`
	Department   string    `orm:"column(department)"`
	Requirements string    `orm:"column(requirements);type(text)"`
	Location     string    `orm:"column(location)"`
	SalaryRange  string    `orm:"column(salary_range)"`
	Status       string    `orm:"column(status)"`
	PostedAt     time.Time `orm:"column(posted_at)"`
}

type SearchResult struct {
	Id       int64
	Name     string
	Title    string
	Location string
	Score    float64
}

// SearchCandidates performs a fuzzy search on candidate profiles.
// - searchTerm: keyword to search for (user input)
// - searchField: field to search in ("full_name", "title", "skills", "summary")
// - matchMode: "contains", "startswith", "endswith", "exact"
// - limit: max results
func SearchCandidates(o orm.Ormer, searchTerm string, searchField string, matchMode string, limit int) ([]SearchResult, error)

// SearchCandidatesBySkills finds candidates with specific skills.
// - skillPattern: skill pattern to match (user input, skills are comma-separated)
// - minExperience: minimum years of experience
func SearchCandidatesBySkills(o orm.Ormer, skillPattern string, minExperience int) ([]Candidate, error)

// SearchJobPostings searches job postings.
// - searchTerm: keyword to search for
// - searchField: field to search in ("title", "requirements", "department")
// - matchMode: "contains", "startswith", "endswith", "exact"
func SearchJobPostings(o orm.Ormer, searchTerm string, searchField string, matchMode string, limit int) ([]JobPosting, error)

// AutocompleteCandidates provides autocomplete suggestions.
// - partialInput: what user has typed
// - field: field to autocomplete from ("full_name", "title", "location", "education")
// - maxSuggestions: maximum suggestions to return
func AutocompleteCandidates(o orm.Ormer, partialInput string, field string, maxSuggestions int) ([]string, error)

// SearchByLocation finds candidates by location pattern.
// - locationPattern: partial location to match (user input)
func SearchByLocation(o orm.Ormer, locationPattern string) ([]Candidate, error)

// AdvancedCandidateSearch performs multi-criteria search.
// - namePattern: name pattern (user input)
// - titlePattern: job title pattern (user input)
// - minExperience: minimum experience
// - status: filter by status, empty for all
func AdvancedCandidateSearch(o orm.Ormer, namePattern, titlePattern string, minExperience int, status string) ([]Candidate, error)
```

**Business Context**:
- Recruiters search candidate database using keywords
- Search supports partial matching (e.g., "java" finds "javascript")
- Skills are stored as comma-separated values
- Table names: `candidate` and `job_posting`

## Code Requirements
- The generated code must use `package main`.
- Only implement the six functions above. Do NOT include main() function.
- Do NOT call orm.RegisterModel() or orm.RegisterDataBase().
- Do NOT include any init() function.
- Use SQL LIKE operator for pattern matching.

## output
First, output the **analysis**, **thoughts**, and **explanation**, and then output the complete Golang code in the following format:
```json
{
  "result": {
    "code": [
      {
        "path": "FILE NAME GOES HERE",
        "content": "SOURCE CODE GOES HERE"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

